# Line endings are not a style preference here, they are a correctness one.
#
# The content tests in all four product sites read `.mdx` frontmatter with a
# pattern that requires an LF, because that is what the files carry in the
# repository. This repository had no `.gitattributes` and `core.autocrlf` is true
# by default on Windows, so a checkout on a Windows machine turned every content
# file into CRLF and the content tests failed on a tree nobody had edited. The
# tests were right and the checkout was wrong; the fix is the one that makes the
# checkout agree with the repository rather than the one that makes the test stop
# looking.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect what a file is and normalise it on the way in, which
# is the only setting that is correct for a repository holding prose, source and
# lockfiles together. The explicit lines below are the exceptions, and each has a
# reason rather than a habit.
#
# MDX, Markdown and the two source trees are text and are normalised to LF. A
# frontmatter block is line-oriented data before it is prose, and a consumer
# reading it with a newline-anchored pattern is reading it as data.

* text=auto

*.mdx text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf

# The lockfile is generated and is read as a graph by the consumer gate kit, which
# parses it rather than grepping it. Normalising it would change bytes nobody
# edited, and its integrity hashes are already the thing that gate is careful not
# to text-scan.

pnpm-lock.yaml -text
